## Claim
Text: "You’re watching the cheaters and all those people that send in the phony ballots. … They want to have the count weeks after November 3."
Claim author: Donald Trump
Claim date: October 30, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### Was there documented evidence of widespread fraudulent or "phony" mail-in ballots being submitted in the 2020 United States presidential election prior to Donald Trump's statement on October 30, 2020?
Answer: No, there was no documented evidence of widespread fraudulent mail-in ballots prior to October 30, 2020. According to the provided source, "Numerous national and state-level studies show that voter fraud is incredibly rare in the US," and Ellen Weintraub, commissioner of the Federal Election Commission, stated that "'There's simply no basis for the conspiracy theory that voting by mail causes fraud.'" While there were "isolated cases of postal-ballot fraud" under investigation, claims of widespread fraud were made "without evidence," and other highlighted issues—such as erroneous ballots in Ohio—were attributed to technology malfunctions rather than fraud.

Source URL: https://www.bbc.com/news/54562611

### What were the official state laws and regulations regarding the legal deadlines for counting mail-in and absentee ballots in the United States for the November 3, 2020, presidential election?
Answer: For the November 3, 2020, presidential election, "many states require that a ballot be received by Nov. 3, however, 21 states and Washington, D.C., will count ballots that arrive after Nov. 3, with deadlines varying." While some states mandated that ballots arrive by Election Day to be counted, others legally allowed ballots to be counted if they were postmarked by Election Day (or the day before) and received within a specified post-election window. These extended receipt deadlines ranged from a few days after the election—such as November 6 in Pennsylvania and Massachusetts—to several weeks later, including November 20 in California and November 23 in Washington.

Source URL: https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/10/30/fact-check-some-mail-ballots-arriving-post-election-day-count/6075311002/

### Did any United States state laws or election procedures in 2020 legally permit or require the counting of mail-in ballots received or postmarked by Election Day (November 3, 2020) to continue in the days or weeks following November 3?
Answer: Yes, in 2020, "21 states and Washington, D.C., will count ballots that arrive after Nov. 3, with deadlines varying." Multiple states legally permitted ballots postmarked by Election Day to be received and counted in the days and weeks following November 3. For example, Illinois allowed ballots postmarked by Election Day to be received up to 14 days later (November 17), while California and Washington allowed ballots postmarked by November 3 to be received and counted as late as November 20 and November 23, respectively.

Source URL: https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/10/30/fact-check-some-mail-ballots-arriving-post-election-day-count/6075311002/

### Is it a standard historical practice in United States presidential elections for official ballot counts to continue and be certified days or weeks after Election Day?
Answer: Yes, it is standard practice in United States elections for official ballot counts to continue and be certified weeks after Election Day. According to the provided text, "election results are often not made official until weeks after Election Day, when election administrators complete an official canvass and certify the results." For example, "in some states like California, election results are not certified for nearly a month," and provisional ballots "often remain uncounted for weeks after the election," typically not being included in the count until the conclusion of the canvass, "often one to four weeks after the election."

Source URL: https://bipartisanpolicy.org/report/counting-the-vote-during-the-2020-election/

### Did election officials or state authorities in the United States intentionally delay the ballot counting process in the 2020 presidential election for partisan or fraudulent reasons?
Answer: According to the provided report, there is no indication that election officials or state authorities planned to delay the ballot count for partisan or fraudulent reasons. Instead, any delays in the 2020 election results were anticipated due to legitimate logistical and legal factors, such as a "massive increase in voting by mail" due to the pandemic, the "time-intensive and often manual process" of verifying and processing envelope ballots, and state laws that prevent officials from beginning to process ballots before Election Day. The report notes that "there is nothing objectively wrong with a slow vote counting process" to ensure accuracy, and warns that "unfounded claims of fraud or misconduct" regarding these delays would only harm voter confidence.

Source URL: https://bipartisanpolicy.org/report/counting-the-vote-during-the-2020-election/

### What security measures and verification processes were in place during the 2020 United States presidential election to detect and prevent the submission of "phony" or fraudulent mail-in ballots?
Answer: According to the provided source, several security measures and verification processes are used to validate mail-in ballots. First, "voters must be registered to vote to request and cast a mail-in ballot." Once submitted, the ballot envelope "typically requires a voter’s signature or other identifying information," which election officials verify by checking "to make sure that the ballot signature matches that voter’s signature on file." Additionally, the ballot must be postmarked, received by an election official, or both, by a specific date set by state law before it can be validated, separated from its envelope, and counted.

Source URL: https://bipartisanpolicy.org/blog/by-mail-or-absentee-voting-is-safe-and-easy-and-those-ballots-count/

### Did independent election observers, law enforcement agencies, or bipartisan election officials find evidence of systemic cheating or organized ballot fraud in the lead-up to the November 3, 2020, United States presidential election?
Answer: No, law enforcement and national security agencies found no evidence of systemic cheating or organized ballot fraud in the lead-up to the 2020 election. According to an August 2020 briefing by high-ranking officials from the FBI, the Department of Homeland Security, and the Office of the Director of National Intelligence, the FBI had "no evidence of any coordinated fraud schemes related to voting by mail" and "no information about any nation state" attempting to undermine mail-in voting. Additionally, state and local election officials affirmed that the decentralized nature of U.S. elections makes such schemes highly difficult, noting that "bad information around the use of mailed ballots is more of an issue than fraud."

Source URL: https://www.npr.org/2020/08/26/906262573/theres-no-evidence-supporting-trump-s-mail-ballot-warnings-fbi-says

### How did the COVID-19 pandemic affect the volume of mail-in ballots and the expected timeline for counting votes in the 2020 United States presidential election?
Answer: Due to concerns about the COVID-19 pandemic, a higher percentage of voters chose to cast their ballots by mail in the 2020 election. This increased volume of mail-in ballots meant that "it will take more time for election officials to carefully process and count all votes than in previous presidential elections," and as a result, "voters should not expect results on election night."

