### Summary of Key Insights
The retrieved evidence demonstrates that there was no systemic or widespread fraud associated with mail-in ballots in the 2020 election. National security agencies, including the FBI and DHS, as well as the Federal Election Commission, found no evidence of coordinated mail-in voting fraud schemes, and the Trump campaign failed to produce any such evidence when legally ordered to do so in court. Furthermore, counting ballots in the weeks following Election Day is a standard, legal, and historical practice in U.S. elections. In 2020, 21 states and Washington, D.C., had laws explicitly permitting ballots postmarked by November 3 to be received and counted in the days and weeks that followed. The extended timeline was a result of legitimate logistical factors, such as a pandemic-driven surge in mail-in voting and rigorous security verification procedures, rather than partisan or fraudulent delays.

### Conclusion
The claim that "cheaters" are sending in "phony ballots" to illegitimately push the vote count weeks past November 3 is explicitly contradicted by established election laws and security findings. Mail-in voter fraud is extremely rare, and security agencies found no evidence of systemic cheating. Moreover, counting and certifying ballots in the weeks after Election Day is a standard, legally mandated process designed to ensure accuracy, especially given the high volume of mail-in ballots in 2020. Because the evidence clearly disproves the assertions of widespread fraud and the illegitimacy of post-Election Day counting, the final decision is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim is refuted because there was no evidence of widespread mail-in ballot fraud, and counting ballots after Election Day is a standard, legal practice. According to the [Federal Election Commission](https://www.bbc.com/news/54562611) and the [FBI](https://www.npr.org/2020/08/26/906262573/theres-no-evidence-supporting-trump-s-mail-ballot-warnings-fbi-says), there were no coordinated fraud schemes, and voter fraud remains incredibly rare. Additionally, the Trump campaign [failed to present any legal evidence](https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/aug/21/trump-campaign-voter-fraud-pennsylvania) of systematic mail-in ballot fraud when ordered to do so in court. Furthermore, [21 states and Washington, D.C.](https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/10/30/fact-check-some-mail-ballots-arriving-post-election-day-count/6075311002/) legally allowed ballots postmarked by Election Day to be counted in the days and weeks that followed. This post-election counting is a [standard historical practice](https://bipartisanpolicy.org/report/counting-the-vote-during-the-2020-election/) to ensure accuracy, and the expected timeline in 2020 was due to a pandemic-driven surge in mail-in voting and rigorous [security verification procedures](https://bipartisanpolicy.org/blog/by-mail-or-absentee-voting-is-safe-and-easy-and-those-ballots-count/), not fraudulent delays.